# modules/dast/passive/checks.rules - the §7.1 cookie-flag script-check
# registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md DAST-06, tier 2).
#
# ONE `checks.rules` FOR THE WHOLE §7.1 TIER, WHICH IS THE FROZEN FORMAT'S CHOICE
# AND NOT THIS TICKET'S. DAST-05..DAST-11 are peers with no ordering between
# them and are being built in parallel, so a per-owning-script pack in this
# directory would have kept each ticket's records to itself; that was this
# ticket's first attempt and it is not available. rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path
# table reserves the basename `checks.rules` repository-wide for the §9.5 schema
# and a record file matching no row of that table is `E070` - measured, on
# `modules/dast/passive/cookies.rules`, by `tests/lint-rules.sh` itself. So the
# tier shares this file. A parallel passive ticket appends its own records here
# and will meet a merge conflict in this file; it is an append-only conflict
# between records that do not interact, so take both sides.
#
# The type tag is `passive`, matching the phase table's
# `passive/cookies.sh:passive` floor (modules/dast/engine.sh). Reading the
# `Set-Cookie` headers of responses to plain GET requests mutates nothing, which
# is the whole of docs/DESIGN.md §7.1's admission criterion.
#
# THREE ATTRIBUTES, FOUR CHECK IDS, AND THE FOURTH IS THE POINT. `SameSite` has
# two failure states that are different facts with different fixes: the attribute
# ABSENT (no policy stated, so the effective one is the visitor's browser default
# - Chromium applies `Lax`, other engines have shipped both `Lax` and no
# restriction at all) and the attribute PRESENT with a value that gives no
# cross-site protection (`None`, or a token no browser recognises). One id could
# only state one of them, and `check_id` is a fingerprint component, so a single
# id would make those two states one finding whose meaning flips between runs.
# The remaining components (target, method, path_template, param_location=cookie,
# param_name=<cookie name>) are what make two flags missing on one cookie two
# findings rather than one.
#
# `intrusive` is deliberately NOT set on any of these: nothing here changes target
# state, so the passive tier is the whole gate and requiring --allow-intrusive on
# top would be a second, undocumented one.

id: DAST-COOKIE-NO_SECURE-01
title: Cookie set without the Secure attribute
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-614
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Set the Secure attribute on every cookie the application issues, so
  a browser only ever sends it over TLS. Serve the site over HTTPS only and add
  HSTS so the first, pre-redirect request cannot carry the cookie either. For a
  session cookie set Secure, HttpOnly and an explicit SameSite together - each
  defends a different attack and none substitutes for another.
references: CWE-614
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-COOKIE-NO_HTTPONLY-01
title: Cookie set without the HttpOnly attribute
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1004
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Set the HttpOnly attribute on every cookie no client-side script
  legitimately needs to read, which for a session or authentication cookie is
  always. If a script genuinely needs a value, put that value in a separate,
  non-authenticating cookie and leave the session cookie HttpOnly. HttpOnly
  limits the damage of a cross-site-scripting flaw and does not fix one.
references: CWE-1004
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-COOKIE-SAMESITE_ABSENT-01
title: Cookie set with no SameSite attribute
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1275
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: State the policy explicitly rather than relying on a browser default
  that differs between engines and changes between releases - SameSite=Lax for a
  session cookie, which still survives ordinary top-level navigation to the site,
  or SameSite=Strict where no cross-site entry point needs the cookie. Only use
  SameSite=None for a cookie a third-party context genuinely requires, and pair
  it with Secure. SameSite reduces cross-site request forgery exposure and is not
  a substitute for an anti-CSRF token on a state-changing request.
references: CWE-1275
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-COOKIE-SAMESITE_WEAK-01
title: Cookie set with a SameSite value that gives no cross-site protection
script: passive/cookies.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1275
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Use SameSite=Lax or SameSite=Strict unless a third-party context
  genuinely needs the cookie. If it does, SameSite=None is only honoured when
  Secure is also set - modern browsers reject a None cookie without it outright,
  so the cookie is silently dropped rather than merely unprotected. Correct an
  unrecognised value rather than leaving it, because browsers do not agree on how
  to treat one. Protect every state-changing endpoint with an anti-CSRF token
  regardless.
references: CWE-1275
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

# DAST-05's block of this shared registry - the §7.1 security-header
# script checks (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md tier 2).
#
# THE BASENAME IS FORCED, AND THAT IS WHY THIS FILE IS SHARED GROUND.
# rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table gives the §9.5 script-check schema to
# "any file named `checks.rules`, at any depth" and makes every other path
# `E070`, so a per-ticket `headers-checks.rules` is not a legal record file.
# DAST-05..DAST-11 are peers built in parallel and each owns its own block of
# records here; the file is append-only between them and a conflict in it is
# resolved by keeping BOTH blocks, never by choosing a side.  This block is
# DAST-05's and its ids are all `DAST-HDR-*`.
#
# `checks_registry_load` (lib/checks.sh) globs every *.rules under
# modules/dast/ at any depth, so this file loads alongside
# modules/dast/active/checks.rules with no registration step.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `passive`, matching the phase table's
# `passive/headers.sh:passive` floor (modules/dast/engine.sh).  Both gates must
# permit and neither widens the other (tension 15's intersection rule); reading
# a response header mutates nothing, so `passive` is the honest tier and
# `intrusive` would be a second, undocumented opt-in for a request a browser
# makes anyway.
#
# ONE CHECK ID PER DEFECT, NOT PER HEADER.  The DAST finding location profile is
# (target, method, path_template, param_location, param_name) and carries no
# component naming the defect, so a single `DAST-HDR-MISCONFIG-01` would make a
# missing CSP and a weak HSTS on the same page collide on one fingerprint and
# dedupe to one finding.  HSTS is three ids for the same reason
# docs/DESIGN.md §7.1 asks for them separately - missing, weak and malformed
# are three different conversations with an operator, and only the third means
# "you configured it and got nothing for it".

id: DAST-HDR-CSP_MISSING-01
title: No Content-Security-Policy on a document response
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1021
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Serve a Content-Security-Policy header on every document response.
  Start from a restrictive base such as `default-src 'self'; object-src 'none';
  frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'`, then widen it one source at a time
  against a report-only deployment. A Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only header
  collects violations but enforces nothing, so it does not close this gap on its
  own.
references: CWE-1021
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-HDR-CSP_UNSAFE-01
title: Content-Security-Policy permits unsafe-inline or unsafe-eval for script
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Remove 'unsafe-inline' and 'unsafe-eval' from the script fetch
  directive. Move inline event handlers and inline `<script>` blocks into files,
  or authorise the ones that must stay with a per-response nonce or a hash - a
  directive carrying a nonce or hash makes browsers ignore 'unsafe-inline'
  anyway, so the two are alternatives rather than complements. Replace
  `eval`/`new Function`/string `setTimeout` so 'unsafe-eval' can go.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-HDR-CSP_DATA_SOURCE-01
title: Content-Security-Policy allows a data: source for script or object content
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Drop `data:` from script-src, object-src and any default-src that
  governs them. A `data:` URI is attacker-authorable in full, so allowing it as
  a script source restores arbitrary script execution that the rest of the
  policy exists to prevent. Where inline data really is needed, keep it to
  img-src or font-src, which this check does not flag.
references: CWE-79
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

id: DAST-HDR-CSP_WILDCARD-01
title: Content-Security-Policy allows a wildcard host for active content
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-942
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Replace the wildcard host with the specific origins the page
  actually loads from. A bare `*` allows any origin on the internet; a
  `*.example` wildcard allows every subdomain, including any one an attacker can
  get control of through a dangling DNS record or a shared hosting tenant.
references: CWE-942
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-HDR-HSTS_MISSING-01
title: No Strict-Transport-Security header on an HTTPS response
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Send `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains`
  on every HTTPS response. Without it, a user's first request of a session - or
  any request after the pin expires - can be downgraded to plaintext and
  intercepted before the redirect to HTTPS is ever seen. Confirm every
  subdomain is HTTPS-ready before adding includeSubDomains.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-HDR-HSTS_WEAK-01
title: Strict-Transport-Security max-age is too short to be protective
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Raise max-age to at least 31536000 (one year), the floor the
  preload list requires. A short max-age narrows the window in which a browser
  refuses plaintext to a few days or hours, and `max-age=0` actively clears an
  existing pin - which is a rollback switch, not a policy. Ramp up gradually if
  the site is still being migrated, then leave it at a year.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-HDR-HSTS_MALFORMED-01
title: Strict-Transport-Security header is present but unusable
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Fix the header so it carries exactly one decimal `max-age`
  directive, for example `max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains`. RFC 6797 makes
  max-age required and tells the browser to ignore the whole header without it,
  so a malformed value gives the same protection as sending nothing while
  looking in a configuration review as though the control is in place.
references: CWE-319
references: https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6797

id: DAST-HDR-CLICKJACKING-01
title: Document response has no framing protection
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1021
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Add `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'` (or a list of
  the origins genuinely allowed to frame the page) and keep
  `X-Frame-Options: DENY` alongside it for older browsers. `ALLOW-FROM` is
  obsolete and is honoured by no current browser, so a policy that relies on it
  is not enforced anywhere.
references: CWE-1021
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-HDR-NOSNIFF_MISSING-01
title: No X-Content-Type-Options nosniff on the response
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-430
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Send `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` on every response. Without
  it a browser may ignore the declared Content-Type and execute a user-uploaded
  or user-influenced file as script or stylesheet on the basis of its bytes,
  which turns an upload feature into a cross-site scripting vector.
references: CWE-430
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-HDR-REFERRER_LEAKY-01
title: Referrer-Policy sends the full URL to cross-origin destinations
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Set `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` or
  `no-referrer`. Under `unsafe-url` or `no-referrer-when-downgrade` the whole
  URL - path, query string, and any password-reset or invitation token in it -
  is sent to every third-party host the page links to or loads a resource from.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-HDR-RECOMMENDED_MISSING-01
title: Recommended security headers are not set
script: passive/headers.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Add the headers named in this finding's evidence. Each is a defence
  in depth rather than the fix for a specific defect, which is why they are one
  finding and not several: Permissions-Policy withholds powerful browser
  features from the document and anything it embeds, the Cross-Origin-*: trio
  isolates the page from cross-origin windows and embedders, and
  X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies stops a legacy client honouring a
  crossdomain.xml this site may not know it serves. The list is data, in
  modules/dast/passive/recommended-headers.txt.
references: CWE-693
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
# --- DAST-11, passive/markup.sh: the §7.1 HTML-markup family ---------------
#
# Appended by the DAST-11 ticket. This file is SHARED by every tier-2 passive
# ticket (see its header): a merge conflict here is append-only between records
# that do not interact, so take both sides rather than choosing one.
#
# The type tag is `passive`, matching the phase table's `passive/markup.sh:passive`
# floor (modules/dast/engine.sh). Every one of these checks is decided entirely
# from the bytes of a response to a plain GET; no form is ever submitted and no
# discovered link is ever followed, which is the whole of docs/DESIGN.md §7.1's
# admission criterion. `intrusive` is deliberately NOT set on any of them.
#
# SIX IDS FOR FOUR REQUESTED CHECKS, AND THE TWO SPLITS ARE THE POINT.
# `check_id` is a fingerprint component, so an id is the only place a DIFFERENT
# FACT ABOUT THE SAME LOCATION can live: two defects on one page under one id
# would collide on one fingerprint and `findings_merge` would silently keep one.
#   - Reverse tabnabbing splits by PAGE SENSITIVITY because this ticket asks for
#     it to be "weighted higher on login and redirect pages", and severity is a
#     per-record property of the registry rather than something an emitting
#     script may invent at runtime - a phase that raised `base_severity` above
#     what its own record declares would put the report and the registry into
#     disagreement, which tests/suites/dast-markup.sh asserts cannot happen.
#     Two ids is how a severity weighting is expressed here.
#   - Insecure framing splits by DEFECT because docs/DESIGN.md §7.1 names two
#     ("framing untrusted or http:// origins"): a plaintext frame is a transport
#     failure fixed by changing a URL, an unsandboxed cross-origin frame is a
#     missing control fixed by adding an attribute and a CSP directive. One id
#     could only carry one remediation.

id: DAST-MARKUP-SRI_MISSING-01
title: Cross-origin script or stylesheet loaded without Subresource Integrity
script: passive/markup.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-353
owasp: A08:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Add an integrity attribute carrying the sha384 (or sha256/sha512)
  digest of the exact file, and a crossorigin attribute alongside it - without
  crossorigin the response is opaque, the browser cannot verify the digest, and
  it blocks the resource instead. Pin the version in the URL as well: an
  integrity hash against a "latest" URL breaks on every upstream release, which
  is what pushes teams to remove the attribute. Where a third party will not
  serve a stable, hashable artifact, self-host the file instead; until then this
  page executes whatever that origin serves it, with full access to this page's
  DOM, cookies and storage.
references: CWE-353
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/

id: DAST-MARKUP-TABNABBING-01
title: Cross-origin target=_blank link without rel=noopener
script: passive/markup.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1022
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Add rel="noopener noreferrer" to every link that opens a
  cross-origin destination in a new browsing context. The opened page receives a
  window.opener handle to this one and can navigate it anywhere it likes, so a
  user who returns to the original tab may be looking at an attacker page at the
  address they expected. Current Chromium, Firefox and Safari imply noopener for
  target=_blank, so this is defence for older and embedded browsers and for any
  code that opens the window with window.open - which is NOT covered by that
  implicit default and needs the noopener feature string passed explicitly.
references: CWE-1022
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-MARKUP-TABNABBING_SENSITIVE-01
title: Cross-origin target=_blank link without rel=noopener on an authentication or redirect page
script: passive/markup.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1022
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Add rel="noopener noreferrer" to every link that opens a
  cross-origin destination in a new browsing context, and treat this page as the
  priority: it is an authentication, credential or redirect page, so the tab an
  attacker can rewrite through window.opener is the one the user is about to
  type a password into or has just been handed control of. rel="noreferrer"
  matters here beyond noopener as well - without it the full URL of this page,
  including any single-use login, reset or continuation token in its query
  string, is sent to the third-party destination as the Referer.
references: CWE-1022
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-MARKUP-FRAME_INSECURE_SCHEME-01
title: Frame or embedded object loaded over plaintext HTTP
script: passive/markup.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Serve the framed document over https and change the src to match. A
  plaintext frame is readable and rewritable by anyone on the network path, and
  whatever the user types into it is too. Where the page itself is HTTPS this is
  active mixed content, which current browsers block outright - so the frame is
  not merely insecure, it does not load, and the feature it provides is silently
  broken. Add a Content-Security-Policy with block-all-mixed-content or
  upgrade-insecure-requests to catch the next one.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-MARKUP-FRAME_UNTRUSTED-01
title: Cross-origin frame embedded without a sandbox attribute
script: passive/markup.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-829
owasp: A08:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Add a sandbox attribute naming only the capabilities the embedded
  document genuinely needs, and constrain what may be framed at all with a
  Content-Security-Policy frame-src directive listing the specific origins. An
  unsandboxed cross-origin frame can navigate the top-level window, open dialogs
  and run plugins in this page's context. Note that sandbox="allow-scripts
  allow-same-origin" together defeats the sandbox for a same-origin document, so
  do not reach for that pair as a default. This check reports the absence of a
  control, not a compromised third party: a frame whose origin you own and trust
  may legitimately carry no sandbox, which is why its confidence is medium.
references: CWE-829
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/

id: DAST-MARKUP-CSRF_TOKEN_ABSENT-01
title: State-changing form served without an anti-CSRF token
script: passive/markup.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-352
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Issue a per-session (or per-form) synchroniser token, render it into
  every state-changing form as a hidden field, and reject any POST whose token is
  absent or does not match. Set SameSite=Lax or Strict on the session cookie as
  well: it is a real second layer, and on its own it covers neither a same-site
  subdomain an attacker controls nor a cross-origin request that carries the
  credential some other way. This check reads only the markup as served, so it
  cannot see a token a script injects at submit time, a double-submit cookie, or
  an Origin/Referer check made server-side - which is why its confidence is
  medium and why a finding here is a prompt to confirm the defence, not proof
  there is none.
references: CWE-352
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/
